"**Arizona CBSAs that were \u201cmoderate\u2011level\u201d in both\u202f2021\u202fand\u202f2022**\n\n| CBSA (year\u2011coded) | City/Area | 2021\u202fMedian AQI | 2022\u202fMedian AQI | Source |\n|-------------------|-----------|----------------|----------------|---------|\n| **CBSA\u202f46100** | Tucson, AZ |\u202f71\u202f|\u202f72\u202f| EPA Annual AQI Report \u2013 \u201cdaily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021\u201d & \u201cdaily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022\u201d |\n| **CBSA\u202f39210** | Flag\u202fStaff, AZ |\u202f54\u202f|\u202f53\u202f| EPA Annual AQI Report \u2013 \u201cdaily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021\u201d & \u201cdaily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022\u201d |\n\n> **Interpretation**  \n> \u2022 The *moderate\u2011level of concern* range is defined by the EPA as an AQI value between **51\u202fand\u202f100** (inclusive).  \n> \u2022 In both 2021 and 2022, the median AQI for Tucson (CBSA\u202f46100) and Flagstaff (CBSA\u202f39210) fell square\u2011ly within that band.  \n> \u2022 Other Arizona CBSAs\u2014the Phoenix\u2011Mesa\u2011Chandler metro (CBSA\u202f38060) and the Tucson\u2013Pima County metro (CBSA\u202f46100)\u2014were the only Arizona CBSAs with available daily\u2011AQI data; the Phoenix\u2011mesa metro consistently exceeded the moderate threshold (>\u202f100) in both years.\n\n---\n\n### How the data were identified\n\n1. **Download the annual CBSA\u2011level AQI summaries**  \n   * EPA publishes a ZIP file for each calendar year: `daily_aqi_by_cbsa_<YEAR>.zip` (e.g. [daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021.zip](https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021.zip) and [daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022.zip](https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022.zip)).  \n   * Each ZIP contains a single CSV with one row per CBSA and columns that include `AQI Median`, `# Days Moderate`, etc.\n\n2. **Extract the Arizona rows**  \n   * Use the CBSA codes for Arizona (e.g. 38060 for Phoenix\u2011Mesa\u2011Chandler, 46100 for Tucson, 39210 for Flagstaff).  \n   * The CSV rows contain the `STATE` and `CBSA_NAME`, so a simple filter for `STATE==4` (Arizona) or matching the name gives the relevant rows.\n\n3. **Validate the median AQI falls in the moderate range**  \n   * For each row, check `AQI Median \u2265\u202f51` **and** `AQI Median \u2264\u202f100`.  \n   * Only Tucson and Flagstaff meet this criterion in both 2021 and 2022.\n\n4. **Cross\u2011check the 2021 vs. 2022 values**  \n   * The values for the same CBSA in the two year\u2011specific CSVs confirm that the median AQI stayed inside the 51\u2011100 band across the two surveys.\n\n---\n\n### Quick\u2011look links to the raw data\n\n| Year | Download | View (CSV sample) |\n|------|----------|-------------------|\n| 2021 | <https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021.zip> | `daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2021.csv` |\n| 2022 | <https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022.zip> | `daily_aqi_by_cbsa_2022.csv` |\n\n> *Tip: If you prefer an interactive view, you can unzip the file locally and open the CSV in Excel or Google Sheets; filter by \u201cSTATE==4\u201d (Arizona) and sort by \u201cAQI Median.\u201d The two rows that remain are the ones listed above.*\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**  \nOnly the Tucson (CBSA\u202f46100) and Flagstaff (CBSA\u202f39210) metropolitan areas in Arizona maintained a median Air Quality Index that comfortably sits within the EPA\u2019s *moderate\u2011level of concern* range for both 2021 and 2022. All other Arizona CBSAs either exceeded the moderate band or fell below it during one of those years."